{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n20 \n \n \n(GMT 301), was carried out with the long (near) thermocouple rake, where all other Flame Design \ntests were conducted with the short (far) rake.  In his request for this additional test day, the PI \nstated in part: \n\u201cThe ability to validate the TFP is very important for most of the ACME experiments.  Thus, \nmeasuring the near-field temperatures under conditions where we have TFP data would \nbe highly desirable, almost required.  \u2026 These results would be the only measurements \nACME has with a direct comparison with TFP, and since we already know exactly which \nflames to run to get good data, we know that we will be successful in obtaining this data \nand validating the TFP data.  \u2026 Reiterating, this extra day would directly benefit all of the \ntemperature data of Flame Design, and all of the spherical flame ACME experiments, by \nproviding at least one direct validation of the TFP method.  As you know, we have \nuncertainties about the accuracy of the TFP technique we are using in the ACME rig, and \nhaving these T/C measurements could have a profound impact on the ACME results.\u201d \n \nAs discussed in the previous section, the decision was made to use the CFI-G radiometer array in \nFlame Design\u2019s second round of testing rather than the standard ACME array that often saturated \nin the earlier testing. \n \nCoiled igniter tips were also used in the second round of testing instead of the loop igniter tips \nused in the first round, where the coiled were believed to be more robust. \n \nOther changes to the test plans were minor in comparison. \n \nB. Overview of Experiment Test Operations \nWhile not yet prepared, additional details will be made available in the ACME/Flame Design \nExperimental Procedure document. \n \nThe narrative description of the experimental procedure is an excerpt from the \u2018Methods / \nExperimental\u2019 section of the following conference paper. \nY.-C. Chien, J. Tinajero, D. Stocker, U. Hegde, and D. Dunn-Rankin, \u201cElectric Field Effects on \nFlames in Microgravity on the International Space Station,\u201d 2018 Spring Technical Meeting of \nthe Central States Section of The Combustion Institute, Minneapolis, MN, 20-22 May 2018. \n \nWhile the paper was written for the Electric-Field Effects on Laminar Diffusion Flames (E-FIELD \nFlames) experiment, much of the discussion applies to ACME in general, including the Flame", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 20}}